{
    "article_summary": "The article describes a method to switch mesoionic carbene (MIC) organocatalysis between radical and ionic pathways by controlling the base strength. Using weak bases like Cs2CO3 favors the formation of Breslow intermediates (BIs) and an ionic pathway for the coupling of aldehydes and alkyl halides to yield ketones. In contrast, strong bases like tBuOK generate Breslow enolates (BI-s), leading to a radical pathway. The ionic method demonstrates broad functional group tolerance, including beta-functionalized alkyl halides and sugar moieties.",
